v2 HMAC-SHA2 secrets but one clock is outside the new key’s active lifetime. What breaks?',['They can select different active keys and fail OSPF authentication','The area automatically becomes stub','The routers fall back to no authentication','BGP HoldTime selects the OSPF key'],0,'Junos keychain activeness is wall-clock based, so time synchronization matters during key rollover.','Correct secret text is insufficient if the active key window differs.','https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/ospf/topics/topic-map/configuring-ospf-authentication.html'),
('Juniper Networks','MPLS explicit-null CoS','packet reasoning','H_J_EXP_COS','An explicit-null label with EXP=5 is popped at the egress. Without explicit-null CoS preservation, what can be lost?',['The MPLS EXP-based classification, causing fallback to payload classification','The BGP route itself','The LDP session','The Ethernet FCS'],0,'Junos explicit-null CoS handling preserves MPLS EXP classification through the pop event.','Explicit-null label presence alone does not preserve QoS classification semantics.','https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/cos/topics/task/cos-applying-mpls-exp-classifiers-for-explicit-null-labels.html'),
('Aruba/HPE','UDLD','scenario diagnosis','H_UDLD','Fiber carrier is Up but one direction cannot receive protocol frames. Which AOS-CX feature directly detects the one-way link?',['UDLD on the physical ports at both ends','Root Guard','Port security','MVRP'],0,'UDLD is designed for unidirectional Layer-2 failures that physical carrier may not expose.','Carrier Up does not prove bidirectional control traffic.','https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.06/PDF/6100/L2%20Bridging%206100%206200.pdf'),
('Aruba/HPE','BPDU Guard vs Root Guard','design','H_BPDU_ROOT','A distribution-facing port should participate in STP but must never accept a superior root claim. Which guard fits?',['Root Guard','BPDU Guard','BPDU Filter','Port security client limit'],0,'Root Guard allows STP participation while preventing a superior root on that boundary.','BPDU Guard is for ports where any BPDU is unexpected.','https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.13/PDF/l2_bridging_8100-83xx-9300-10000.pdf'),
('Aruba/HPE','Port Security','configuration review','H_PORTSEC','Global port security is enabled but one access port still learns unlimited MACs. Which dependency remains?',['Enable port security on that specific port','Enable OSPF passive on the VLAN','Configure Root Guard','Set MVRP registration fixed'],0,'AOS-CX requires both global feature availability and per-port enforcement configuration.','System-level enablement is not proof of interface enforcement.','https://developer.arubanetworks.com/new-central-config/reference/readportsecurity'),
('Multivendor','EVPN Type-2 vs Type-5','architecture','H_EVPN_TYPES','A fabric advertises a host MAC/IP endpoint and a routed /24 external prefix. Which route types map to them?',['Type 2 for MAC/IP; Type 5 for IP prefix','Type 3 for MAC/IP; Type 1 for IP prefix','Type 4 for MAC/IP; Type 2 for IP prefix','Type 5 for MAC/IP; Type 3 for IP prefix'],0,'Type 2 carries MAC/IP advertisements; Type 5 carries IP-prefix routes.','Separate endpoint signaling from routed-prefix signaling.','https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/evpn.html'),
('Multivendor','VXLAN MTU','packet reasoning','H_VXLAN_MTU','A 1500-byte tenant packet fails after VXLAN encapsulation while smaller packets work. What changed?',['Outer VXLAN/UDP/IP headers made the transmitted packet exceed the underlay MTU','The VNI becomes invalid at exactly 1500 bytes','EVPN withdraws the MAC when DF is set','LACP changes the packet length'],0,'Encapsulation adds bytes; the underlay must accommodate tenant MTU plus outer headers.','Always evaluate the encapsulated packet size, not only the inner payload.','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/td/docs/switches/lan/catalyst9500/software/release/26-x/configuration_guide/vxlan/26x-bgp-evpn-vxlan-9500-cg/configuring_evpn_vxlan_integrated_routing_and_bridging.html'),
('Multivendor','Redistribution loop prevention','design','H_REDIST_TAG','OSPF routes are redistributed to BGP at Border A and can return BGP→OSPF at Border B. What prevents feedback while preserving unrelated BGP routes?',['Mark the OSPF origin at the first boundary and deny that mark on return redistribution','Raise OSPF Hello timers','Lower every BGP MED','Disable next-hop-self'],0,'Origin metadata carried through policy allows only feedback routes to be rejected at the return boundary.','Loop prevention needs durable route-origin marking.','https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/routemap.html'),
('Multivendor','EVPN DF election','packet reasoning','H_EVPN_DF','Why does an all-active EVPN Ethernet Segment still elect a DF?',['To coordinate BUM forwarding toward the shared segment and avoid duplicate delivery','To select the only PE allowed to advertise Type-2 routes','To elect the route reflector','To choose the underlay IGP DR'],0,'All-active unicast does not remove the need to coordinate BUM forwarding onto a multihomed segment.','DF is a segment/BUM coordination role, not a BGP RR role.','https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/evpn.html'),
('Multivendor','BGP recursive next hop','packet reasoning','H_RECURSION','A BGP route is best and resolves through an IGP route, but ARP/ND for the final gateway is unresolved. Which object blocks forwarding?',['The final unresolved adjacency at the end of recursion','The route distinguisher','The BGP community set','The OSPF area type'],0,'Recursive selection must terminate in a usable adjacency/tunnel for the FIB to forward.','A valid chain of RIB routes is not enough if the final adjacency is incomplete.','https://docs.frrouting.org/en/latest/bgp.html'),
('Multivendor','MST region digest','scenario diagnosis','H_MST_DIGEST','Two switches have identical MST name/revision but different VLAN-to-instance mappings. Why are they in different regions?',['The VLAN mapping contributes to the MST configuration digest','LACP system priority is different','The OSPF router ID differs','The BGP AS number differs'],0,'MST region identity includes the VLAN-to-instance map in the digest.','Name and revision alone do not define the same region.','https://www.arubanetworks.com/techdocs/AOS-CX/10.13/PDF/l2_bridging_6300-6400.pdf'),
('Multivendor','SD-Access LISP/VXLAN','architecture','H_SDA','In SD-Access, which control/data-plane split is correct?',['LISP provides endpoint/control mapping; VXLAN carries fabric data-plane encapsulation','VXLAN provides endpoint mapping; LISP carries Ethernet frames','LISP replaces ISE policy; VXLAN replaces OSPF','Both are management-only'],0,'Cisco SDA uses LISP for control-plane mapping and VXLAN for data-plane transport.','Keep mapping/control and packet encapsulation roles separate.','https://www.cisco.com/c/en/us/solutions/enterprise-networks/software-defined-access/index.html'),
('Multivendor','OSPF NSSA translation','protocol mechanics','H_NSSA','An ASBR inside an NSSA originates an external route. How does it reach a normal area?',['An eligible ABR translates Type 7 to Type 5','The DR converts it to Type 2','The ASBR originates Type 3 directly into every area','BGP converts it to EVPN Type 5'],0,'NSSA external reachability uses Type 7 internally and ABR translation to Type 5 toward normal areas.','Remember the special external LSA boundary transformation.','https://www.juniper.net/documentation/us/en/software/junos/ospf/topics/topic-map/configuring-ospf-route-control.html'),
]
for r in hist:
    v,d,typ,m,st,ch,ans,why,miss,src=r; add(v,d,typ,m,st,ch,ans,why,miss,src,'spaced repetition')
